---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";

const effectiveDate = "September 14, 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How teacher.dev handles your data, including access to Google Drive files you choose to share."
  compactContent
>
  <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl">
      Privacy Policy
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">Effective {effectiveDate}</p>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-8 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
      <div class="border-l-4 border-slate-300 bg-slate-50 p-5">
        <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900">The short version</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          We never see your files. When you connect Google Drive, you pick
          specific files, and those files move directly between your browser and
          Google. Our server holds the permission that keeps that connection
          alive — not your documents, not their names, not a list of them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Who we are</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          teacher.dev ("we", "us") builds tools for teachers. This policy covers
          teacher.dev and its subdomains, including the sign-in and
          authorization service at auth.teacher.dev.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">
          What we access in your Google Account
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          When you sign in with Google, we receive your email address and a
          Google account identifier. That is all we request to identify you.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          When you connect Google Drive, we request a single narrow permission,
          called <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1 py-0.5 text-sm">drive.file</code>.
          It grants access <strong>only</strong> to files you explicitly select
          through Google's file picker, or that the app creates on your behalf.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          We do not request, and cannot obtain, access to your Drive as a whole.
          Files you have never selected are not visible to us or to the app —
          not their contents, not their names, not their existence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">What we store</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">On our server we keep:</p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>Your Google account email address and account identifier</li>
          <li>The list of permissions you actually granted</li>
          <li>
            An encrypted credential that lets us renew your authorization
            without asking you to sign in again
          </li>
          <li>
            A short-lived encrypted access token, cached for up to an hour
          </li>
          <li>
            A record of authorization events — when you connected, when access
            was renewed, when renewal failed, and when access was revoked
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">What we do not store</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          We do not store the contents of your files, their names, or any list
          of your files. We do not copy your documents to our servers. When the
          app reads or writes a file you selected, that request goes directly
          from your browser to Google and does not pass through us.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Why we store it</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Google access expires after about an hour. Holding the renewal
          credential is what lets you connect once instead of re-authorizing
          every session. We use it for nothing else.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">
          How long we keep it, and how to delete it
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          We keep your authorization until you disconnect or delete your
          account. Disconnecting revokes the permission at Google and deletes
          the stored credential from our server. Deleting your account revokes
          every connected permission first, then removes your account data.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          You can also revoke our access at any time, independently of us, at
          <a
            href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions"
            class="font-semibold text-slate-900 underline underline-offset-4 decoration-2"
            >myaccount.google.com/permissions</a
          >. If you do, the app will simply stop working until you reconnect.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Who we share it with</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Nobody. We do not sell, rent, or trade your information, and we do not
          share it with advertisers or data brokers. We do not transfer data
          received from Google APIs to third parties.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">
          Google API Services User Data Policy
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          teacher.dev's use and transfer of information received from Google
          APIs to any other app will adhere to the
          <a
            href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
            class="font-semibold text-slate-900 underline underline-offset-4 decoration-2"
            >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
          >, including the Limited Use requirements.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">How we protect it</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Renewal credentials are encrypted before they are written to disk,
          using a key stored separately from the database. All traffic is served
          over HTTPS. Session cookies are inaccessible to page scripts. Your
          browser never receives the long-lived renewal credential — only a
          short-lived token scoped to the files you picked.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          No system is perfectly secure, and we do not claim otherwise.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">
          Students, classrooms, and schools
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Teachers may use our tools with files that contain student
          information, such as a grade book. Those files stay in the teacher's
          own Google Drive. We do not read, copy, or retain their contents, and
          we do not build profiles of students.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          teacher.dev is intended for use by educators, not by children. If your
          school or district requires a data agreement before staff use
          third-party tools with student records, please contact us before
          adopting teacher.dev.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          If we change this policy we will update the effective date above. If a
          change materially affects how we handle your data, we will make a
          reasonable effort to tell you before it takes effect.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Contact</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Questions about this policy, or a request to delete your data, can go
          to
          <a
            href="mailto:privacy@teacher.dev"
            class="font-semibold text-slate-900 underline underline-offset-4 decoration-2"
            >privacy@teacher.dev</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
